Revision history of WwW-GenGenLib


0.01_00
    *** Perl 5.8 is required, and so is Encode::Locale.
    *** File names are character strings: read_make, write_make,
        update_file, load_state, read_deps and walk_deps encode
        them to the file system locale (locale_fs), and die if
        a name cannot be spelled there.  Pass decoded names; a
        UTF-8 byte string will be encoded a second time.  What
        is in the files is UTF-8, as it always was.
    + to_fs, un_fs: the same, for anyone else (un_fs is
      for what the system gives back: getcwd, glob, ...).
    *** read_deps: a header line is any comment (after anything
        at all), blanks, a field name of letters, `.', `_' or
        `-', and a colon.  Fields not ours (USE:, vim:, Have:,
        and NEED-ONLY: for now) are skipped: they neither end
        the header nor begin it.  Any number of blanks may
        follow the comment and surround the colon; REM must be
        a word of its own.
    *** read_deps: names are split at blanks (space and tab)
        only: a no-break space, form feed and so on are part
        of a name.  A CR at the end of a line is not.

0.00_03  2026-11-08  RC
    + norm_deps: pass_thru => 1 preserves unrecognized dollar
      syntax (for Makefile.in's dependency parameter expansion
      syntax).  Strict mode still takes precedence.

0.00_02  2026-11-07  RC
    !!! Because these are very new functions, they are
        unstable and subject to change!  Please don't
        use them in place of your own policy/logic yet :(

    + enroot: path concatenation; basically like
      File::Spec::Unix->catfile, but made so that
      subs_deps({ B => $Bdir, p => $path }, '${B}/${p}')
      is enroot($Bdir) . $path, and enroot($Bdir, $path)
      is the same unless $path is absolute (absolute
      paths are explicitly distinguished from relative
      paths.)  Right paths are NOT normalized! namely
      empty segments and dot segments are preserved.
    + uproot: quasi-inverse of enroot; with similar
      discrimination for absolute paths.  This path is
      NOT always safe to use as a relative path! Since
      slashes following the prefix are kept.  It is
      caller's responsibility to strip them or reject.
    + norm_deps: HAVE NEED WANT header normalization.
      It accepts a { strict } option deciding how it
      should handle stray "$" characters: lenient (replace
      them with a literal one; the default) or strict (die).
    + subs_deps: basic permissive variable expansion.  A
      variable that is a whole path segment followed by a
      slash is a directory, and is planted like enroot does:
      it keeps one trailing slash, and "." or empty vanishes
      (${a}/times.html is times.html when a is ".").  Else
      it is just text; nothing special about "." there.
      (So good-${a}/times.html is good-./times.html, not
      good-times.html.)
    + split_dep: return a pair of (symbolic root, path).
    + walk_deps: list pooled dependencies, hard or soft,
      recursively.  The default behavior implements some
      sane protection against possibly malicious project
      file headers, such as requiring that the relative
      path segment to not contain ../.. chains.  On top
      of that a dependency in the build directory (${B})
      is not followed, not out of precaution but because
      it'd be wrong to conflate *project* file headers
      with header-resembling output in the build tree. ;)
      This behavior may be fine-tuned with the { follow }
      keyword argument: override completely or fall back
      to &follow_ok, the default implementation.
    - Remove dependency on parent.pm.

0.00_01  2026-11-04  RC
    * read_deps learns to give up on the first malformed UTF-8
      line, so binary files like image assets, fonts, etc. are
      not scanned (erroneously) for a header.

0.00_00  2026-10-31  RC
    Migration from WwW-GenLib?  I'll copy the change log
    from there:

    * read_make/write_make/update_file/load_state: MY/config.mk
      and MY/option.mk, written only if they changed (mtime
      kept, so reconfiguring does not rebuild the world).

    *** BREAKING interface from WwW-GenLib 0.00_00!
        -> write_make (and the update_file that implements it)
           now returns a number instead of a string.

                | genlib-0.0000  | gengenlib-0.0000
         -------+----------------+----------------------
           new? | "created"      | 2
           chg? | "updated"      | 1
           none | ""             | 0

        -> load_state returns a flat hash ( config => \%D,
           option => \%O ) instead of a pair ( \%D, \%O ).
           to assign the old names in list context anyways:

             my %S = load_state($Bdir);
             my ($D, $O) = @S{qw( config option )};

           or in one line:

             my ($D, $O) = @{+{ load_state($Bdir) }}{qw( config option )};

    * read_deps: HAVE/NEED/WANT headers in any comment style,
      REM and :: included.  Paths are from the project root
      and are expanded by siteconf (see its docs).

    * parse_base: sanitize site_url and base_url.

      This is also a port, with the special handling of `/'
      base_url patched by Claude too.
